<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Future Careers – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Future Careers</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> I Want to Be a Doctor Because...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Get the gist of dialogues in social settings. Interactive: Participate in group problem-solving sessions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can get the gist of dialogues about career ideas by identifying main goals (e.g., "What does Mariana want to be?" "A teacher."). Can participate in group discussions on future goals, showing comprehension by contributing ideas (e.g., "What can help you become a doctor?" "Studying hard."). Can answer questions in problem-solving discussions about careers (e.g., "What does Liam plan to do next?" "He will study engineering.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays pictures of various professionals (e.g., a doctor in a hospital, a lawyer in a courtroom, a teacher in a classroom) on the board. Students listen as the teacher names each career. Then, the teacher asks students to identify other jobs they know and what skills might be important for them (e.g., "What do doctors do?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher points to a picture of a doctor and says, "I want to be a doctor because I want to help people." The teacher then points to a teacher and says, "I would like to be a teacher because I enjoy explaining things." The teacher models the pronunciation of target vocabulary: <i>doctor, lawyer, teacher, career, goals, plan, professional, skilled, important</i>, and the modals <i>should, would, will</i>, paying attention to the /ʊ/ and /ɪ/ sounds. The teacher also models "What do you want to be in the future?" and "I plan to study after school."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'I want to be a pilot,' am I talking about the past or the future?" (Future)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a 'career' a hobby or a job you train for?" (A job you train for)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Future Careers")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An audio recording of two 8th-grade students, Maria and Liam, discussing their future career goals and plans. The dialogue includes expressions like "I want to be a doctor because...", "I will need to...", "I would like to...", and uses vocabulary such as <i>doctor, lawyer, teacher, career, goals, plan, internship, qualifications, prepare</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Get the gist of dialogues in social settings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First Listening (Gist):</b> Students listen to the dialogue once. The teacher asks, "What are Maria and Liam talking about?" and "What is the main topic of their conversation?" (Answer: Their future career plans/goals).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second Listening (True/False):</b> Students listen again and decide if statements related to the dialogue are true or false. E.g., "Maria wants to be a doctor." (True) "Liam plans to be a teacher." (False, he plans to be an engineer).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Third Listening (Information Matching):</b> Students listen and match each speaker to their specific career goal and one reason or plan (e.g., Maria → Doctor → Help people; Liam → Engineer → Build things).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice listening for specific details using Wh-questions for career goals, future tense for plans ("I will need to..."), modals for intention and suggestions ("I would like to..."), and "want to + infinitive." Focus on target vocabulary: <i>doctor, lawyer, teacher, career, goals, internship, qualifications, plan, practice, train, achieve, succeed, prepare, professional, skilled, important</i>. Pronunciation practice of modals: <i>should, would, will</i> (/ʊ/ and /ɪ/) and minimal pairs like /ʃ/ and /tʃ/ (e.g., "wish" vs. "witch" if applicable, or in context of *professional*). </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Listen & Fill-in-the-Blanks):</b> The teacher distributes a transcript of the audio dialogue with key vocabulary words (e.g., <i>doctor, plan, goals, professional</i>) and modal verbs (e.g., <i>will, would</i>) blanked out. Students listen to the audio again and fill in the missing words. Teacher pauses after each blank to allow students to write and check their listening accuracy.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice (Listen & Complete Sentences):</b> Students receive incomplete sentences based on the dialogue. E.g., "Maria ______ to be a doctor because she ______ to help sick people." Students listen to the audio one more time and complete the sentences, focusing on the future tense and "want to + infinitive." They also identify and underline any modals used for suggestions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Check (Listening for Accuracy):</b> In pairs, students compare their filled-in transcripts and completed sentences. They re-listen to specific parts of the audio if they disagree, to confirm their answers. The teacher circulates, providing support for pronunciation of modals and minimal pairs and clarifying any confusion in comprehension.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Future Careers")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Problem-Solving Discussion focusing on active listening and comprehension.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher divides students into small groups of 3-4. Each group receives a "problem card" with a short scenario about a fictional peer who has a career goal but faces a challenge. For example: "Carla wants to be a successful lawyer, but she doesn't like reading. What should she do?" or "Roberto wants to be a skilled engineer, but he doesn't know what subjects to study."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One student in the group reads the problem aloud. The other students listen carefully to understand the career goal and the challenge.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After listening, group members take turns suggesting solutions, expressing intentions, and asking follow-up questions to clarify what they heard. Examples: "Carla wants to be a lawyer. She will need to practice reading more. Maybe she could find books about law or history. What do you plan to do next?" or "Roberto needs to know what subjects are important for engineering. He should talk to a professional."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The focus is on listening to the problem, understanding it, and contributing relevant ideas, using phrases like "I think she should...", "He will need to...", "I would suggest...", and using target vocabulary (<i>goals, plan, train, prepare, achieve, succeed</i>). This activity directly addresses "Participate in group problem-solving sessions" and "providing suggestions for improvement".</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in the group problem-solving discussion from Stage 4. The teacher uses an observation checklist to assess students' listening comprehension and their ability to contribute relevant ideas based on what they heard.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Gets the gist of the problem scenario.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Contributes relevant ideas showing comprehension of the problem.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (e.g., <i>career, goals, plan, prepare</i>) intelligibly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses "want to + infinitive," future tense, and modals (<i>should, would, will</i>) appropriately.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Actively listens and responds to peers' suggestions.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students write their answers on a small slip of paper.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One new career word I heard today is __________.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy or difficult about listening to other people's career plans and problems?</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How well did I understand the different career goals and plans today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What can I do to improve my listening skills for future discussions about careers?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students were able to get the gist of the dialogues and identify main goals. The group problem-solving activity encouraged active listening and relevant contributions. Some students struggled with distinguishing similar-sounding modals like <i>would</i> and <i>should</i>. I noticed students had good engagement with the scenario. For the next lesson, I will reinforce the pronunciation of modals and ensure more opportunities for students to verbalize their comprehension of listening tasks.</p>
                <p style="text-align="justify" style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In our next lesson (Lesson 2), we will continue exploring "Future Careers" by reading a text that uses the same vocabulary we learned today. This will help us see these words in a new context and deepen our understanding.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>